  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 300 PM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Chances for showers and storms tonight into Tuesday \nas a slow-moving front lingers across the area.\n\nThis afternoon, a nearly stationary boundary is situated along or \njust south of the VA/NC border. Mostly cloudy to overcast skies have \ndeveloped for areas just north of the front with northeasterly flow \ntaking hold. As expected there is a significant temperature gradient \nacross the forecast area, with readings in the upper 60s across far \nNE portions of the area (Ocean City) to around 90 across our far \nsouthern locations. A majority of the area is seeing temperatures in \nthe upper 70s to mid 80s this afternoon. As for the severe weather \nthreat this afternoon/evening, SPC still has western portions of the \narea in a Marginal Risk for severe weather, but the best chances for \nany stronger or severe storms is likely across our far SW \n(Lunenburg, Mecklenburg, Brunswick) where we have seen the most \nsun/have the most instability. Showers and storms should increase in \ncoverage later this afternoon into this evening, becoming more \nwidespread during the overnight hours. Lows tonight will be in the \nmid to upper 60s for most everyone.\n\nOn Tuesday, the Canadian high pressure shifts a bit eastward, \nserving as a reinforcement of our onshore flow. Northeast winds will \nbegin to pick up along the Eastern Shore Tuesday morning, then \nspreading to all coastal locations through the day. Widespread \n(coastal) gusts to 25mph will be likely, with gusts increasing to 35 \nto up to 45 mph along the Atlantic coast of the Eastern Shore \n(including Ocean City, Chincoteague, etc) Tuesday night into \nWednesday. The lingering front will keep rain chances going on \nTuesday for most of the area. Could get some isolated storms as well \nthat create locally heavy downpours and instances of flash flooding. \nWPC has highlighted the western half of the area in a Day 2 Slight \nERO. Highs will range from the upper 60 N-NW to the low-mid 80s \nacross southern portions of the area. The cooler airmass will make \nits presence known starting Tuesday night as lows will be in the mid \n50s across the northwest, with low to mid 60s inland and upper 60s \nalong the SE and coastal areas. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Persistent onshore flow and a strengthening coastal \nlow will create a wetter, cooler, and breezier forecast for most of \nthe week.\n\nCold air damming will overtake the area on Wednesday as high \npressure remains parked to our north. This will keep high \ntemperatures in the low to mid 60s for the northern and western \ninland areas, with low to mid 70s for the SE half of the CWA. Winds \nfor coastal areas will persistently be blowing around 20-25 mph with \ngusts to 35+ mph. The strongest winds will once again be along the \nAtlantic coast of the Eastern Shore with gusts to 40-45 mph likely \nTuesday night, potentially lasting all the way through at least \nThursday as the low pressure remains offshore. Rain chances continue \neverywhere on Wednesday, though due to the stable cooler \nenvironment, storms aren't expected, with the exception of the NC \ncoast. There could once again be areas of locally higher rainfall at \ntimes that lead to flash flooding. WPC has a Marginal Risk of \nExcessive Rainfall for a majority of the area on Wednesday. Rain \nchances may focus more across the eastern portions of the area later \nThursday into Friday. A secondary surface low may form and cause the \nmain low to recenter itself slightly closer to the Mid-Atlantic/New \nEngland coast late Thursday into Friday. Highs will remain in the \n60s with 70s closer to the coast both day. If winds are able to \nshift more out of the north around the west side of the low \npressure, our low temperatures Thursday and Friday night could dip \ninto the mid 50s, with the potential for even some upper 40s well \ninland. \n\nInto the weekend and beyond, models are showing that this low may \ncontinue to hang around to our northeast. As of right now, it still \nappears that it will be just far enough from our area that we may \nhave a pleasant weekend for most of the area, but that definitely \ncould change as the details of the low's placement come to light. Be \nsure to stay tuned to the latest forecast updates as this system \nwill create a prolonged period of gusty onshore winds, coastal \nflooding, high surf, beach erosion, and dangerous marine conditions \n(see below). \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Coastal Flood Watches have been issued for a large \nmajority of the tidal area starting Wednesday afternoon/evening high \ntide as widespread moderate to locally major tidal flooding \n(inundation of 2-3 feet AGL in vulnerable areas) is likely to strong \nnortheast winds. Tidal flooding is expected to continue for several \ntide cycles (potentially through the end of the work week) given the \npersistent northeast winds.\n\nStrong northeast winds are expected from Tuesday night through at \nleast Thursday, which will cause water levels to rise across much of \nthe area (especially",
